Q: Write a program that keeps taking in integers until a negative integer is given. Then take all the positive integers and get the average of them all A: <pre><code>package Basics;

import java.util.Scanner;
public class ComputeUntilNegative {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(average());

        }

    // Compute the average of numbers until the user enters a negative number.
    public static int average() {
        Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);
        int sum = 0;
        int i = 0;
        int averag = 0;
            while(true) {
                int num = scan.nextInt();

                if(num &lt; 0) {
                    try {
                    averag = sum / i;
                    }catch(ArithmeticException e ){
                        System.out.println(&quot;Can't devided by zero.&quot;);
                            }
                    return averag;
                    }
                    else {
                    sum = sum + num;
                    i++;
                    }

            }

    }








}
</code></pre>

Q: When can the address of immediate function be used as template argument? A: <p>As for the problem you’re trying to solve, one workaround is a zero-cost wrapper class:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;concepts&gt;

consteval void foo() noexcept {}
class FooWrapper {
    public:

    static consteval void operator()() noexcept
    {
        foo();
    }
};

template &lt;std::invocable&lt;&gt; F&gt;
struct  A{ int i = 1; };

consteval auto bar() {
    return A&lt;FooWrapper&gt;{};
}
</code></pre>
